-Description: privately derive native DisplayID RGB BT.2020/PQ for the 0.3 ABI
- Adapt upstream private CTA context/lifecycle and validated DisplayID2
- native interface features. Keep installed headers, exported interfaces,
- struct layouts, SONAME and project version unchanged. Nested CTA joins
- ordinary CTA in first-match extension order; native fallback is atomic
- when neither CTA HDR nor colorimetry exists. Type 1 and luminance are not
- fabricated from native signal support. Standalone APIs are excluded.
+Description: expose opt-in parsed DisplayID2 capability on the private 0.3 backport
+ Adapt historical upstream private CTA context/lifecycle and proposed parsed
+ DisplayID2 interface features. Add four parsed getters/types, preserving
+ existing ABI layouts, SONAME and project version. Legacy high-level getters
+ retain native-only defaults; ordinary and nested CTA retain the prior private
+ first-match extension ordering. Native capability consumers must opt in to
+ the low-level API and validate complete tuples and CTA authority themselves.
+ No Type 1 or luminance is fabricated from native signal support.
  .
- Local reviewed ownership repairs release partly initialized nested state
- on actual allocation failure, preserving ENOMEM. A narrow conservative
- difference from upstream4ad suppresses only native fallback for identified
- unusable nested CTA: unsupported revision/flags, incomplete 0x81 header,
- outer or child truncation, empty extended tags and rejected HDR/colorimetry.
- Complete earlier CTA fields remain authoritative; unrelated warnings and
- valid empty/audio-only CTA do not suppress native support.
+ Local ownership repairs release partly initialized nested state on actual
+ allocation failure, preserving ENOMEM. Private malformed-container tracking
+ from the earlier backport remains internal. Unsupported nested CTA parent
+ revisions/flags are now diagnosed and skipped, including empty/audio-only
+ parents. Complete earlier children of supported parents remain available.
  .
- Regression/fault tests use static wrapped objects and unchanged 0.3 APIs.
- Only the private static test copy and allocation executable disable LTO,
- retaining optimization/hardening; installed library and ABI consumer keep
- production LTO. Link-time wrapping otherwise hides counter side effects.
- This local detection build does not enable GNOME HDR: Mutter and i915's
- separate missing-Type1 signaling requirements remain unresolved.
+ Public-API, legacy-header, regression and allocation-fault tests accompany
+ the adaptation. Only private static fault-test objects and the allocation
+ executable disable LTO; installed library and ABI consumers keep production
+ optimization/hardening/LTO. Link-time wrapping otherwise hides counters.
+ This library API is one prerequisite of a separately reviewed Mutter/i915
+ native-HDR stack; it does not by itself enable live HDR.
